# NEXUS .gitignore
#
# §BUILT 2026-07-13 — this codebase had no version control at all before
# this commit. That's the real root cause behind "two sessions independently
# built real, conflicting work without knowing about each other" — every
# session worked from an isolated zip snapshot with no way to see what
# changed elsewhere, and reconciling two sessions' work meant manual,
# line-by-line archaeology (which is what most of one long session was
# actually spent on). Git doesn't prevent two sessions from touching the
# same file — nothing can prevent that without real-time coordination — but
# it makes divergence *visible immediately* (a `git status`/`git log`
# check costs nothing) and gives real merge tooling instead of eyeballing
# `diff -rq` output across an entire tree by hand.
#
# §HONEST SCOPE ON WHAT'S EXCLUDED — only things directly confirmed as
# accumulated runtime state (event logs, sigma records, memory stores that
# grow on every boot). Where a data file's purpose was unclear (e.g.
# baseline-events.ndjson — could be a real fixture/seed file referenced by
# tests, not just accumulation), it's left TRACKED rather than guessed at.
# Untracking something that turns out to be pure noise is a one-line fix
# later; accidentally excluding a real fixture file and not noticing for a
# while is a worse failure mode. Same "when uncertain, don't guess"
# discipline as everything else in this tree.

node_modules/
*.log
.DS_Store

# Confirmed accumulated runtime state — grows on every boot, not source:
guardian/memory_store/*.jsonl
guardian/memory_store/*.json
cortex/data/memory/*.json
data/cortex/memory/*.json
data/ledger/*.jsonl
idearium/data/idearium-repos.json
writeback-failures.jsonl
tests/brutal-results.json
tests/integration-results.json

# Per-system runtime data directories — confirmed live accumulation via
# direct inspection this session (event logs, not fixtures):
data/*/event_log.jsonl
data/*/ledger.jsonl

# Idearium's own generated spec instances (each one is a real user
# artifact, not source code — these belong in the running deployment's
# data, not the codebase's history. If this project wants example specs
# tracked as fixtures, track them explicitly by path, not via this
# directory wholesale).
idearium/data/specs/*/

# Electron/build artifacts, if/when they appear
dist/
build/
*.asar
